  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows a deep upper low just off of the \nEastern Seaboard with another upper low coming onshore over Central \nCA. Meanwhile, weak upper ridging is in place through the \nPlains and last but not least, Polo is still churning just off \nof southern Baja. \n\nJust like early Sun morning, we are looking for another round of fog \nacross northwest into central KS this morning. The main \nquestion will be how far east the more dense fog makes it and \nfor now will keep with the thinking that locations along and \nwest of Highway 14 will have the best chance to see the lower \nvisibilities. \n\nStill looking for weak upper ridging to keep things dry across the \narea today. By 12z Tue, upper low will be moving into the \nDesert Southwest and will allow very rich moisture to stream \nnorth from the Gulf and overspread the Southern/Central Plains. \nAt the same time, Polo looks to quickly get pulled northeast \nacross northern Mex. Bulk of the precip is expected to stay to \nour northwest for Mon night into Tue morning with showers and \nstorms starting to affect the forecast area by Tue afternoon \ninto Tue night, with the most widespread activity northwest of \nthe KS Turnpike. The main driver for this round of heavy \nrainfall will be tied to strong 850mb convergence with a strong \nsurge of mid level moisture late Tue night as the \nenergy/moisture from Polo moves into western/central KS. \n\nBy 12z Wed, upper low will be centered over western NM with a sfc \nlow near the OK Panhandle and a surface trough extending from \neastern NE into the surface low. This area of convergence will \ncontinue to be a focus for heavy rain. However, as the upper \nlow continue to push east into the High Plains, a secondary area\nof heavy rain will develop Wed afternoon through Wed night. \nThis will be associated with a rapid increase in 850-700mb \nmoisture transport which will be focused from southern OK into \nsoutheast KS/sw MO. Models remain consistent in showing extreme \nPW amounts for the Tue-Thu time frame which leads to high \nconfidence in very high rainfall rates and risk for flooding \nwith the more intense convection.\n\nECMWF has come into somewhat better agreement with the GFS regarding \nhow the upper energy is handled beyond Thu. They both now pull some \nof the energy northeast and shear it out as a shortwave trough \nslides across Southern Canada into Ontario. This would push the \nbetter precip chances south of the forecast area for Fri afternoon \nthrough the weekend. Previous runs of the ECMWF were lingering \nthe upper energy much further north to start the weekend, which \nwould result in a much wetter forecast. This will be something \nto keep an eye on as models could still change.",
